notes: "That led me to the file format. PowerPoint already keeps slides as separate parts inside the .pptx package; I'm not claiming it has no structure. But the structure is packed inside one file and the working interface is the screen. I wanted the slide to be a page in a folder: a manifest, one file per page, media beside it, history underneath. That's PPTD. I first met the idea in the Kimi Slides ecosystem; the open-source open-slide project gave me pages as React components. HUANDENG is the two things put together in an Electron editor. It is not a renamed PPTX."
